Project Wycheproof (src/test/resources/vectors/wycheproof/)

src/test/resources/vectors/wycheproof/testvectors_v1/ holds 28 unmodified files
from the testvectors_v1/ directory of Project Wycheproof, commit
3fa63dd0344abb611f1fb1d77e119938603ea230 (https://github.com/C2SP/wycheproof).
SOURCE.txt in src/test/resources/vectors/wycheproof/ records where the files
came from, and MANIFEST.sha256 beside it their checksums. Fuzz seeds derived
from those files (strings, keys and signatures copied out of them, each
signature's hex decoded to its octets) are under fuzz/src/test/resources/, each
in a file whose name starts with "wycheproof-".
The files and the seeds are used only by tests and are not in the published
JAR. The upstream material is

  Copyright 2016-2026 The Wycheproof Authors

and is licensed under the Apache License, Version 2.0, the license Revetsec
itself uses (see LICENSE). The upstream LICENSE file at that commit is copied
verbatim to src/test/resources/vectors/wycheproof/LICENSE. The upstream
repository has no NOTICE file.

Examples from IETF RFCs (test sources, test data and fuzz seeds)

Some test sources, test data files and fuzz seeds contain examples, keys and
test vectors copied or derived from IETF RFCs, each cited where it is used:
RFC 4231 section 4; RFC 4648 section 10; RFC 5869 appendix A; RFC 6749 section
4.1.2 and appendix B; RFC 7515 appendix A; RFC 7517 appendix A; RFC 7520
sections 3 and 4; RFC 7638 section 3.1; RFC 8032 section 7.1; RFC 8037 appendix
A; and the example dates of RFC 9110 section 5.6.7. None of them is in the
published JAR.

RFC 4231 (2005) and RFC 4648 (2006) carry an Internet Society copyright notice,
"Copyright (C) The Internet Society", rather than an IETF Trust one, and are
subject to BCP 78 as it stood when each was published. The other RFCs are
Copyright (c) the IETF Trust and the persons identified as the document
authors, all rights reserved, and are subject to BCP 78 and the IETF Trust's
Legal Provisions Relating to IETF Documents
(https://trustee.ietf.org/license-info) in effect on the date each was
published. RFC 8032 was published in the Internet Research Task Force (IRTF)
stream, and section 4 of the Legal Provisions, which covers Code Components and
the Revised BSD License, does not apply to IRTF documents (version 5.0, section
8.g). Its section 7.1 test vectors are copied unmodified, with attribution,
under the license of section 3.a, which section 8.c applies to IRTF documents
without its limit to the IETF Standards Process. The rest of the RFCs with an
IETF Trust notice were published in the IETF stream, and to the extent that
